T2  - International Journal of Infectious Diseases
AB  - Background: The early identiﬁcation of patients at risk of severe dengue infection (DI) is critical to guide clinical management. Nonstructural antigen 1 (NS-1), immunoglobulin M (IgM) and immunoglobulin G (IgG) are used routinely for the diagnosis of DI. We sought to evaluate whether positivity for NS-1, IgM and IgG individually or together is predictive for severe complications of DI.
Methods and materials: With institutional ethics board approval, a prospective study was conducted among patients with DI admitted Nawaloka Hospital Sri Lanka (NH) from 2014 to 2019. DI was diagnosed based on a positive NS1 or IgM. IgG was also tested on all the patients. Clinical data was obtained from electronic medical records at NH. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 20.
Results: We collected data on 3504 patients with DI. There were 2749 (78.5%) cases of DI with no warning signs, 478 (13.6%) with warning signs and 277 (7.9%) with severe DI. Patients who were positive for NS1, IgM and IgG (triple positive: TP) were more likely to develop severe DI (63.8%) in comparison to those who were only NS1 positive (single positive: SP) (3.0%) and patients with positive NS1 and IgM (double positive: DP) (7.5%) [p = 0.001]. Severe symptoms as well manifestations of severe DI were signiﬁcantly more common in TP patients (p = 0.01). Thrombocytopaenia (platelet less than 50 × 109/l), leucopaenia, haemoconcentration, transaminitis and cardiac impairment were all more prominent in TP patients (p = 0.001). Regression analysis conﬁrmed that having a TP status on admission was predictive of severe complications later in the admission (p < 0.01). Receiver operator characteristic curve (ROC) analysis showed (AUC: 84.8; sensitivity = 90.7 and speciﬁcity = 83.2) that TP status on admission had a strong predictive value of thrombocytopaenia in day 5. Among 11 deaths in our cohort, 8 were TP patients.
Conclusion: We propose that TP status on admission is a novel predictive factor for severe DI. Further studies are required to validate our ﬁndings and prospective clinical trials are called for to optimize the early identiﬁcation and management of high risk DI.
